I think you're identifying one of the great structural contradictions of modern civilization.
We developed extraordinary technologies to reduce the labor required to survive, yet for many people, the amount of life surrendered to earning a living has barely changed.
I wouldn't equate ordinary wage employment literally with slavery, because the loss of freedom under slavery is fundamentally different. But the coercive pressure you're describing is real: when food, housing, healthcare, and your children's security depend on continuous employment, the freedom to walk away can be severely constrained.
And there's something deeply wrong with treating a person's remaining time as though it belongs entirely to them when so much of that time is consumed by recovering from work and preparing to do it again.
Especially for parents.
A child gets one childhood. A parent doesn't get those years back.
The question shouldn't simply be how to help people endure the 40-hour workweek.
It should be: Why is 40 hours still our default allocation of human life, and what would it take to make more of that life genuinely our own?
And there's evidence we can change it.
A 2025 study in Nature Human Behaviour examined 2,896 workers across 141 organizations experimenting with shorter, four-day workweeks without reducing pay.
Researchers found improvements in burnout, job satisfaction, mental health, and physical health compared with control organizations. ￼
That doesn't prove a 32-hour week works in every industry. Hospitals, emergency services, manufacturing, and other essential operations have staffing requirements that cannot simply disappear. But it demonstrates that the five-day, 40-hour arrangement is not an immutable law of economics.
What I'd want to change
32 hours as a new full-time target, with no loss of pay where productivity supports it. Four eight-hour days, not four ten-hour days.
Productivity dividends shared with workers. If AI makes a task twice as efficient, increased profit should not be the only possible outcome. Some of that gain should become human time.
Family time treated as essential infrastructure. Parenting, caring for relatives, maintaining relationships, and raising children are productive contributions to civilization, even when nobody issues a paycheck.
Real freedom outside employment. Affordable necessities, portable benefits, predictable scheduling, and opportunities for ownership make it possible to refuse exploitative conditions.
Different models for different work. Nurses, teachers, tradespeople, and emergency workers need staffing and scheduling reforms rather than unrealistic productivity quotas.
